// ================================================================
// GIFT Format Cheat-Sheet + Full Example Bank for your advanced digital systems quizzes
//
// Encoding: The file must be saved in **UTF-8** encoding (not UTF-16 or “Unicode” in some editors).  
// Blank lines: At least one blank line must be left between each question (questions cannot have internal blank lines).  
// Comments: Lines starting with // are ignored by the import filter.  
// Question Title (optional): Use ::Title:: before the question text. If omitted, the question text becomes the title.  
// Question Text: Plain text follows the title (if any).  
// Format tag: Optional [html], [markdown], [plain], [moodle] can precede question text to set formatting mode (default is [moodle]).  
// Answer block: Begins with { and ends with }. Inside are the answers or definitions, depending on type.  
// Control symbols inside the answer block:
//   =  : marks a correct answer for MC or short answer.
//   ~  : marks a wrong answer for MC, or separates multiple answers/options.
//   %n% : used before an answer to specify weighting (e.g., %50% means 50% credit) for partial credit on MC/multi‐answer or numeric questions.
//   #  : used after an individual answer option to provide feedback for that option.
//   For numeric questions: #answer:tolerance (accept ± tolerance) or #low..high (accept any number in the range).
//   Matching: format =left -> right for each pair inside the braces.
//   True/False: use {T} or {F} (or {TRUE}/{FALSE}).
// Escaping special characters: If you need to include a literal {, }, =, ~, # in your question text or answer text (i.e., not as syntax), prefix with backslash: \{ \} \= \~ \#
// New-line inside question text: Use “\n” if you must represent an internal line break (avoid blank actual lines, since blank lines separate questions).
// Category directive (optional): Use $CATEGORY: path/name to declare category for following questions; should be on its own line, and preceded and followed by a blank line.
// ================================================================

// Example Bank Starts Here

$CATEGORY: Advanced Digital Systems / Programming

This is a Descriptions / Stimulus question type used to provide instructions or context for a set of questions.


$CATEGORY: MC

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Multiple Choice – Single Correct (Simple)
::MC_single_simple::What is the binary representation of decimal 5? {
=101 #Correct!
~100 #No — 100 is 4 in binary.
~110 #No — 110 is 6 in binary.
~111 #No — 111 is 7 in binary.
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Multiple Choice – Single Correct with Feedback
::MC_single_feedback::In Verilog, the keyword ‘always’ is used to describe which kind of block? {
=sequential block #Yes — ‘always’ is usually used for sequential logic.
~combinational block #No — combinational logic typically uses always @(*) or assign.
~initial block #No — initial blocks execute at simulation start only.
~continuous assignment #No — continuous assignments use “assign”, not “always”.
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Multiple Choice – Multiple Correct (Select All That Apply) (Simple)
::MC_multi_simple::Which of the following are logic gates? (Select all that apply) {
~%-100%adder #Adder is a device composed of gates, not a basic logic gate.
~%50%AND #Correct — AND is a logic gate.
~%50%OR #Correct — OR is a logic gate.
~%-100%multiplexer #Multiplexer is a circuit, not a basic logic gate.
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Multiple Choice – Multiple Correct with Partial Credit & Feedback
::MC_multi_partial::In a CPU pipeline, hazards may include\: (Multiple correct) {
~%33%structural hazard #Yes — structural hazard is a pipeline hazard.
~%33%data hazard #Yes — data hazard is a pipeline hazard.
~%33%control hazard #Yes — control hazard is a pipeline hazard.
~%-100%thermal hazard #No — this is not typically a pipeline hazard in that context.
####Feedback\: Structural, data, and control hazards are correct pipeline hazards; thermal is unrelated.
}

$CATEGORY: TrueFalse

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// True/False (Simple)
::TrueFalse_simple::In digital logic, a NAND gate gives output 0 only when all inputs are 1. {T}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// True/False with Feedback
::TrueFalse_feedback::A D flip-flop changes output immediately when the input changes. {F#No — the D flip-flop changes only on the clock edge (assuming edge-triggered).}

$CATEGORY: ShortAnswer

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Short Answer (Simple)
::ShortAnswer_simple::Name the ‘father of computing’ often attributed to inventing the Analytical Engine. {=Charles Babbage =Babbage}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Short Answer (Multiple Acceptable Answers)
::ShortAnswer_multiple::In Linux, the default command line editor for many distributions is {=vim =Vi IMproved}

$CATEGORY: MissingWord

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Missing Word (Fill in the Blank) – Simple
::MissingWord_simple::The OSI model has {~seven =seven} layers.

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Missing Word (Fill in the Blank) – Variant
::MissingWord_variant::In Verilog, the non-blocking assignment operator is {~\= =<\=}.

$CATEGORY: Matching

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Matching – Simple
::Matching_simple::Match the programming language to its typical paradigm. {
=Python -> object-oriented
=Haskell -> functional
=C -> procedural
=Prolog -> logic
}

$CATEGORY: Numerical

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Numerical – Tolerance
::Numerical_tolerance::What is the value of π (pi) to three decimal places? {#3.142:0.001 ####Acceptable range ±0.001}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Numerical – Range
::Numerical_range::What is the allowed voltage range for TTL logic high (Voh) in standard TTL? {#2.4..5.0 ####Accept any number between 2.4 and 5.0}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Numerical – Partial Credit Using Weights
::Numerical_partial::In a pipelined processor, the ideal CPI (cycles per instruction) is 1. If you observe a CPI of 1.25, what percentage performance overhead is that? {#
=25:0 #Correct — 25 percent overhead.
=%50%20:5 #Close — 20 percent gives half credit if within ±5.
}

$CATEGORY: Essay

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Essay (Manual grading)
::Essay_simple::Discuss the trade-offs between synchronous and asynchronous digital circuits. {}

// ================================================================
// End of Bank


// ================================================================
// Extra Notes (included here for your students / your workflow)
//
// 1. Saving & Encoding: Always save the file as plain text with UTF-8 encoding. If you only use ASCII characters, ANSI might work, but use UTF-8 to be safe with special characters.
// 2. No blank lines inside a question: A blank line signals the start of a new question. If you need a line‐break inside a question text, use “\n” instead of a blank actual line.
// 3. If you include special control characters in your question or answer text (such as {, }, ~, =, #) and you intend them literally (not as part of GIFT syntax), you must escape them with a backslash: \{ \} \= \~ \#
// 4. Feedback: The #feedback after an answer option applies to that answer. For example, in MC questions you can provide immediate feedback tied to each choice. Short answer answer‐lines can also include #feedback. True/False may allow one feedback for wrong, one for right (depending on LMS support).
// 5. Weights / Partial Credit: In multiple-answer MC (select all that apply) you can prefix answer options with %n% to indicate the percentage of credit if chosen. Negative weights (e.g., %-100%) penalize choosing incorrect options. For numeric questions you can also use weights for alternative answers with =%50% etc.
// 6. Numeric Questions: syntax options:
//      • #answer:tolerance — accepts correct answer ± tolerance.
//      • #low..high — accepts any number between low and high inclusive.
//      • =%n%answer:tolerance — multiple numeric answers with different weights, where %n% indicates percentage credit for selecting that numeric answer (within tolerance).
// 7. Matching Questions: Provide pairs of the form =left -> right inside the braces. At least 3 pairs recommended by many LMSs.
// 8. Missing Word: You embed the {answer set} at the location in the sentence where the blank should appear. The system will display a blank line in the student view. You may still supply multiple potential answers inside the braces (prefixed by = or ~) just like short answer style.
// 9. Category Directive ($CATEGORY): If you include $CATEGORY: path/name before a set of questions, the import will place those questions into that category (or create it). Use blank lines before and after the directive to avoid parsing issues.
// 10. Use of formatting tags: If you need to control how the question text is interpreted (html/markdown/plain/moodle), you can prefix question text with [markdown], [html], etc. This helps if you have code blocks or want markdown formatting inside the question.
// 11. Media support (images/audio): Basic GIFT format doesn’t embed media directly. If your LMS supports it (e.g., via plugin), you may include references like <img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/…" /> or similar. (See “GIFT with medias” plugin as example).
// 12. File sanity: Use a text editor (e.g., vim, neovim) to author this format. Avoid Word or other rich‐text editors that introduce non‐plain text formatting / smart quotes. Test import with a small set of questions first.
// ================================================================

